TERM SHEET SUNDAY
Sunday, March 22, 2026

The week in venture, for founders who read the documents.

QUARTER-END DYNAMICS
The last two weeks of a quarter are when funds close deals they want to mark in the quarter's reporting. Founders in process right now should know two things. Investors are more willing to sign this week than they were three weeks ago, and less willing to negotiate; the trade is speed for terms. If you have a term sheet you like, sign it. If you have one you do not, the leverage to change it improves on April 1, when the clock resets and the partner has time to argue.

THE PREFERENCE STACK, EXPLAINED ONCE
Each priced round adds a class of preferred stock, and each class wants to be paid back before common in a sale. By the Series C a company can have four classes with different preferences, different seniority and, if someone was careless at the B, participation. The founder's share of a $200M exit can vary by tens of millions depending on how the stack was built three rounds earlier. The defense is boring: insist on pari passu (all preferred paid together, not in order of round) from the A onward, and refuse participation at every round, even when it is offered as a trade for a higher headline valuation.

LPS ARE TIRED
Two large university endowments told a conference this week that they are cutting venture allocations for 2027 because distributions have not kept up with capital calls for four years. This is the pressure behind the secondaries boom and behind the specialists' rise: a specialist fund with a clear thesis and modest size is an easier re-up than a generalist mega-fund with a long tail of 2021 marks. For founders, the practical effect is that funds are more sensitive to time-to-liquidity than they were, and will ask about it.

ROUNDS THIS WEEK
- Cobalt Ridge's fund IV (see Wednesday's Extra). Three more specialists are said to be in market.
- A robotics company raised a $75M B at a valuation below its 2022 A. It is fine; the company is better.
- Seed rounds were quiet; quarter-end is not a seed phenomenon.

See you next Sunday.

